    {
      "@id": "urn:uuid:4f75bdf1-62f6-4027-a3f4-b3ef47129364#question",
      "@type": "mira:Question",
      "description": {
        "@type": "Item",
        "format": "text/plain",
        "content": "Is it the case that d1 and D2 receptors encode dopamine signals on distinct temporal scales due to their different binding kinetics: D1 receptors (high EC50, fast on-rate) follow extracellular DA with millisecond delay and act as detectors of transient burst events, while D2 receptors (low EC50, slow off-rate) integrate the dopamine signal over seconds and cannot resolve brief pauses or closely spaced bursts?"
      },
      "haak:autoDerived": true,
      "haak:reviewNote": "Derived mechanically from the hypothesis text; MIRA requires a Question node and the claim tree has none. Needs human review before publication."
    },
    {
      "@id": "urn:uuid:08b37324-70f4-4ff9-bf37-c73d2b8a43c4#question",
      "@type": "mira:Question",
      "description": {
        "@type": "Item",
        "format": "text/plain",
        "content": "Is it the case that dAT nanoclustering acts as a possible regulator of effective DAT activity in striatum: when transporters are concentrated into dense nanoclusters rather than uniformly distributed, local depletion at the cluster surface creates a diffusion-limited bottleneck that lowers the effective Vmax even at constant total DAT expression — proposing nanoclustering as one candidate mechanism for the lower effective Vmax in VS than DS?"
      },
      "haak:autoDerived": true,
      "haak:reviewNote": "Derived mechanically from the hypothesis text; MIRA requires a Question node and the claim tree has none. Needs human review before publication."
    },
    {
      "@id": "urn:uuid:49eb1a86-3a61-4e0f-aa8e-f5c225b9df09#question",
      "@type": "mira:Question",
      "description": {
        "@type": "Item",
        "format": "text/plain",
        "content": "Is it the case that the qualitative regional contrast in extracellular dopamine dynamics between dorsal and ventral striatum — DS producing varicosity-scale hotspots with no pervasive tonic baseline versus VS producing diffuse tonic-like coverage — is principally explained by a difference in DAT Vmax (~3:1 DS:VS), rather than by differences in release-related parameters (vesicular content, release probability, active terminal fraction, or pacemaker firing rate)?"
      },
      "haak:autoDerived": true,
      "haak:reviewNote": "Derived mechanically from the hypothesis text; MIRA requires a Question node and the claim tree has none. Needs human review before publication."
    },
    {
      "@id": "urn:uuid:2ec8fd67-7f5e-432f-b400-4e608348a74e",
      "@type": "mira:Evidence",
      "title": "d1r-tracks-da-50ms-delay",
      "description": {
        "@type": "Item",
        "format": "text/plain",
        "content": "D1R occupancy closely tracks extracellular DA with approximately 50 ms delay during burst firing; D1R occupancy is negligible during pacemaker activity because the EC50 of 1000 nM far exceeds tonic [DA] of ~10 nM in DS, making burst events the effective threshold for D1R engagement."
      },
      "sourceDocument": {
        "@id": "https://doi.org/10.7554/eLife.105214",
        "@type": "SourceDocument"
      },
      "observationBase": {
        "@id": "https://zenodo.org/record/17664800"
      },
      "haakx:epistemicStatus": "mira:Claim",
      "haak:role": "empirical",
      "haak:epistemicStrength": "moderate",
      "haak:relations": {
        "dissociates-with": [
